+OPEN (Laird, 2026-11-16): whether rules may *compute* with numeric metadata is left open,
+as a topic for discussion among the CMC authors. Conditions are blind to it under both options.
+- Option A, strict: numeric metadata reaches behaviour only through selection, retrieval and
+  learning, as the prose above says. Rule actions would then need a further well-formedness
+  condition, because `Rule.act : WM σ → WM σ` can now read and write metadata. Sigma could not
+  be an instance: its conditionals multiply and summarize functional values.
+- Option B, narrow (Rosenbloom, 2026-11-16): rules may not match on numeric metadata, but
+  procedural memory may compute functions on it. This is what `WFProc` already states (it
+  constrains `cond` only); the prose above would change. Sigma can be an instance if its
+  matching depends only on which tuples are present (not yet checked). Metadata then reaches
+  working memory through rule actions too, and can influence later selections.
 -/
 def MetaBlind (c : WM σ → Prop) : Prop :=
   ∀ w₁ w₂ : WM σ, symView w₁ = symView w₂ → (c w₁ ↔ c w₂)
